# Contributing to {{.App.Label}}

This file is yours: esquadro wrote it once and leaves it alone.

## Branches: {{if eq $m "gitflow"}}GitFlow{{else if eq $m "github_flow"}}GitHub Flow{{else if eq $m "gitlab_flow"}}GitLab Flow{{else if eq $m "trunk"}}trunk-based development{{else}}Release Flow{{end}}
{{if eq $m "gitflow"}}
- `{{$main}}` is what is in production: it changes only by releases and hot
  fixes, and each change is a version tag (`v1.2.0`).
- `{{.Options.develop}}` is where work comes together, for the next release.
- `feature/<name>` and `bugfix/<name>` start from `{{.Options.develop}}` and go back into it.
- `release/<X.Y.Z>` starts from `{{.Options.develop}}` to prepare a version: only
  fixes go into it. It goes into `{{$main}}`, with its tag, and back into `{{.Options.develop}}`.
- `hotfix/<X.Y.Z>` starts from `{{$main}}` to fix production, and goes into both.

```sh
task feature:start -- login     # feature/login, from {{.Options.develop}}
task feature:finish             # merges it into {{.Options.develop}}
task release:start -- 1.2.0     # release/1.2.0
task release:finish             # into {{$main}} as v1.2.0, and back into {{.Options.develop}}
task hotfix:start -- 1.2.1      # hotfix/1.2.1, from {{$main}}
task hotfix:finish
```
{{else if eq $m "github_flow"}}
- `{{$main}}` can always be deployed.
- Each change is a short branch from `{{$main}}` (`feature/<name>`), merged by a
  pull request once its CI is green and it is reviewed.
- A version is a tag on `{{$main}}`: `git tag -a v1.2.0 -m v1.2.0`.

```sh
task feature:start -- login     # feature/login, from {{$main}}
```
{{else if eq $m "gitlab_flow"}}
- `{{$main}}` is where work comes together, by pull requests from short branches.
- The environment branches, {{range $i, $e := strs .Options.environments}}{{if $i}}, then {{end}}`{{$e}}`{{end}}, each
  hold what runs there: code moves down by promotion, never up.

```sh
task feature:start -- login     # feature/login, from {{$main}}
task promote -- {{index (strs .Options.environments) 0}}         # {{$main}} into {{index (strs .Options.environments) 0}}
```
{{else if eq $m "trunk"}}
- Everyone commits to `{{$main}}`, small and often, at least daily.
- Work that is not ready hides behind a feature flag instead of a long branch.
- `{{$main}}`'s CI must stay green: fix or revert at once.

```sh
task sync                       # rebase your commits on {{$main}}
```
{{else}}
- Work goes into `{{$main}}` by pull requests from short branches.
- Each version is a branch cut from `{{$main}}`, `releases/<X.Y>`, deployed and
  tagged from there. A fix lands in `{{$main}}` first, then is cherry-picked
  into the release branches that need it.

```sh
task feature:start -- login     # feature/login, from {{$main}}
task release:cut -- 1.2         # releases/1.2, from {{$main}}
task release:pick -- <commit> 1.2
```
{{end}}
The tasks never push: each one says what to send. `task github:protect`
makes GitHub require pull requests{{if uses "github_actions"}} and a green CI{{end}} on the long-lived
branches.

## Commits

Write commit messages as [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org):
`feat: ...`, `fix: ...`, `docs: ...`.
